# Multi-agent system standards

## Agents

- One agent, one job. An agent's description says when to use it and what it
  returns, in two sentences.
- Agents are configuration (prompt, tools, model, limits) registered in one
  place, not classes with bespoke control flow. Adding an agent must not require
  touching the orchestrator.
- Orchestration is explicit code or a declared graph. No agent decides the
  topology at runtime unless an ADR says so.
- Every run has hard limits: max turns, max tokens or cost, wall-clock timeout.

## Tools

- A tool is a typed function: Pydantic input model, Pydantic output model, a
  docstring written for the model that will call it.
- Tools return errors as data the agent can act on; they do not raise through
  the loop.
- Tools with side effects are idempotent or take an idempotency key, and declare
  whether they need human approval.

## Prompts

- Prompts live in versioned files under `prompts/`, not inline strings. A prompt
  change is a code change: reviewed, and covered by an eval.
- Model names and provider settings come from config, never literals in code.

## Evals and observability

- Each agent has at least one eval in `evals/` that fails when its core
  behaviour regresses. A prompt or model change runs the evals before merge.
- Every model call and tool call is traced with a run id, the agent name,
  token counts and latency.
- Shared state between agents goes through one typed store, not ad-hoc globals
  or message-passing by string.
